Component pattern:// Individual components - no height constraints
function MyComponent() { return ( &lt;div className="space-y-4"&gt; {/* Content flows naturally, parent handles scrolling */} &lt;/div&gt; );
}
❌ Avoid:// Don't set heights on components in scrollable containers
&lt;div className="h-full overflow-y-auto"&gt; // ❌ Conflicts with parent scrolling
&lt;div className="min-h-screen"&gt; // ❌ Forces tall content
✅ Use:&lt;div className="min-h-[400px]"&gt; // ✅ Minimum height for centering
&lt;div className="space-y-4"&gt; // ✅ Natural flow
For "coming soon" features:function PlaceholderView({ icon: Icon, title, description, features }) { return ( &lt;div className="flex items-center justify-center min-h-[400px]"&gt; &lt;div className="text-center max-w-md"&gt; &lt;Icon className="w-16 h-16 mx-auto mb-4 text-gray-600" /&gt; &lt;h2 className="text-2xl font-semibold mb-2"&gt;{title}&lt;/h2&gt; &lt;p className="text-gray-400 mb-4"&gt;{description}&lt;/p&gt; &lt;ul className="text-left text-sm text-gray-500 space-y-1"&gt; {features.map(feature =&gt; ( &lt;li key={feature}&gt;• {feature}&lt;/li&gt; ))} &lt;/ul&gt; &lt;/div&gt; &lt;/div&gt; );
}
Problem: Setting h-full or min-h-screen on components inside scrollable containers prevents scrolling.// ❌ Wrong
&lt;main className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto p-6"&gt; &lt;div className="min-h-screen"&gt; {/* Forces parent to be full viewport height */} {content} &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;
Solution: Remove height constraints from children, let parent handle scrolling.// ✅ Correct
&lt;main className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto p-6"&gt; &lt;div className="space-y-4"&gt; {/* Natural flow */} {content} &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;
Problem: Nested scroll containers create confusing scroll behavior.// ❌ Avoid nested scrolling
&lt;main className="overflow-y-auto"&gt; &lt;div className="overflow-y-auto h-full"&gt; {/* Nested scroller */} {content} &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;
Solution: Single scroll container at parent level.// ✅ Single scroller
&lt;main className="overflow-y-auto"&gt; &lt;div&gt; {content} {/* Parent scrolls */} &lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/main&gt;
Problem: Removing component padding after parent already provides it.Rule: Main content area (&lt;main&gt;) provides p-6. Components shouldn't add more.// App.tsx provides padding
&lt;main className="flex-1 overflow-y-auto p-6"&gt; // ❌ Component adds duplicate padding
&lt;div className="p-4"&gt; {/* Now has 6 + 4 = 10 units of padding */} // ✅ Component inherits parent padding
&lt;div className="space-y-4"&gt;
Problem: Parent section doesn't highlight when child is active.Solution: Check if any child is active when determining parent state.const sections = [ { id: 'workspace', children: ['playlists', 'library', 'sessions'] }
]; // Check if any child is active
const isParentActive = (section) =&gt; { return section.children.some(childId =&gt; activeView === childId);
}; &lt;button className={clsx( 'section-header', (expandedSections.has('workspace') || isParentActive(workspaceSection)) &amp;&amp; 'bg-gray-800' )}
&gt;
Problem: Using arbitrary color values instead of Tailwind's semantic classes.// ❌ Hardcoded
&lt;div style={{ backgroundColor: '#1F2937' }}&gt; // ✅ Semantic
&lt;div className="bg-gray-800"&gt;
